 2. Purpose
 ----------
 The ultimate in financial business, The MoneyBin demonstrates brand
 new ways to calculate your CNet incomes. Mainly it tells you how much
 money you have received from your dear (current) users. It also
 calculates the imaginary price you're taking for every megabyte users
 download. Upload/download-ratio determination is also available.


 3. Requirements
 ---------------
 Surprise! You need AN AMIGA!
 CNet 3.05c ... Earlier may do, not tested
 Sort and Delete commands somewhere on your path


 4. Installation
 ---------------
 Just like every other Arexx program. Consult your CNet's owner manual.
 After it's there, change some things from the "MoneyBin.rexx"-file
 with a text editor. You probably want to change the amount users pay you,
 and the accessgroup of those ah-so-supportive users.


 5. Usage
 --------
 Just run it. Program will create two ASCII-bulletins to your gfiles:
 directory and display several statistics. Check those two files with
 your favourite text editor and you'll get the point. You can ofcourse
 add those bulletin-files to your gfiles-menu if you like to. For example,
 your users will surely like to make comments based on those facts...
 like: "that fucking bitchy sysop has got 104834834734$ from us! and
 I'm broke... FFFFFFFFFFUCKING BAAASTARD!"

 If you wish to do several statistics (Perhaps you'd like to know which
 accesslevel gives you most money?) about different access levels,
 just change the variables in main executable. The MoneyBin always
 overwrites the last bulletins IN CASE they are of the same accesslevel
 that's specified in the main exec.

 For birdbrains: The MoneyBin writes files called MB.stats.? and
                 MB.stats.?.user where ? is the accesslevel you've specified.


